<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sora, Lazio</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sora is a town on the Liri river in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Originally a Volscian town, it changed hands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Samnites during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-samnite-war">Second Samnite War</a> and received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> colony in 303 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period it was the seat of a duchy on the border between the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Papal States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sora-lazio.jpg" alt="Sora, Lazio" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vonvikken · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_comune_of_Sora_(province_of_Frosinone,_region_Lazio,_Italy).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sora lies in the province of Frosinone, built on a plain on the banks of the Liri. It is 115 km from the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and 138 km from the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and its municipality borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>. Above the town, on a precipitous rock 540 metres high that guards the valley of the Liri and the entrance to the Abruzzi, stand remains of polygonal walls, where possibly the citadel of the original Volscian town lay, together with remains of medieval fortifications.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> captured Sora in 345, 314 and 305 BCE before they confirmed its annexation by a colony 4,000 strong in 303 BCE. In 209 BCE it was one of the colonies that refused further contributions to the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. By the lex Julia it became a municipium, and under Augustus it was colonised by soldiers of the legio IV Sorana, which had mainly been enrolled there.</p><p>In the early Middle Ages Sora lay in the Ducatus Romanus under the authority of the pope, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> of Gisulf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> captured it in 705. The castle of Sorella on the rocky height above the town was a stronghold of some note. In 1229, during the War of the Keys, Sora submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> and was then sacked by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, who had its inhabitants hanged.</p><p>In 1443 King Alfonso of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> made Sora the seat of an independent duchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> seized it, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> restored it to the Cantelmi, and it ultimately passed to the duke Giovanni della Rovere. Francesco Maria I della Rovere defended the city against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>. Pope Gregory XIII bought it for 11,000 ducats and placed it under his son Giacomo Boncompagni, the first duke of Sora of that family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sora is a town and comune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a> and the seat of some important manufacturing, especially paper mills. The original cathedral, consecrated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-iv">Pope Adrian IV</a> in 1155, was destroyed by the earthquake of 1634. Among the town's churches are the Sanctuary of the Madonna della Figura and San Silvestro Papa. Caesar Baronius, the cardinal and ecclesiastical historian, was born there, as was the film director Vittorio De Sica.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sora, Lazio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Privernum fell in 329 BCE and <b>Sora</b> in the following decades, and by about 304 BCE the Volsci had been absorbed.</blockquote><a href="/state/volsci">Volsci</a></li></ul></section>
